{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 918 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e57d9d637e657425699bba834b25e214e43f83d987a45ab09e2d1f24518005dc" :edb/db-before-t 917 :edb/db-after-t 918 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534230 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:01:45.927Z" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186133878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415103 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186133878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415104 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186133878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415106 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186133878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415107 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186133879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415105 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186133996 1050 900.0 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186134059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130510 13194139534230 17592186045417 false] [17592186134059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415100 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186134059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186134059 1071 "Let $f(x, y) = x^{3} + y$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t) = ⟨3t, t^{3}⟩$ for $t ∈ [0, 1]$, evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}∇f \\cdot dr$." 13194139534230 17592186045417 false] [17592186134059 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨3t,{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186134059 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(3,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(3,1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{3}+1)-({0}^{3}+0) \\\\  & =(27+1)-0 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186135084 1064 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b].$ The fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x = f(b) - f(a).\n$$\n\nA similar result, known as **the fundamental theorem for line integrals,** exists for line integrals of conservative vector fields. This theorem can be stated as follows:\n\n*Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ Then,*\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll discuss how this theorem is proved in simple cases at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's take a look at a concrete example of how to apply this theorem." 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186135084 1064 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b].$ The fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x = f(b) - f(a).\n$$\n\nA similar result, known as **the fundamental theorem for line integrals,** exists for line integrals of conservative vector fields. This theorem can be stated as follows:\n\n> *Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ Then,*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n> $$\n\nWe'll discuss how this theorem is proved in simple cases at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's take a look at a concrete example of how to apply this theorem." 13194139534230 17592186045417 false] [17592186135128 1064 "Here, we prove the fundamental theorem of line integrals in the two-dimensional case. The three-dimensional case is similar.\n\nLet $C$ be a smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function of two variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ We wish to show that\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nUsing our usual formula for calculating line integrals, we can write the left-hand side as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}∇f\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the integrand above is simply the derivative of $f(\\mathbf r(t))$ with respect to $t.$ Thus, we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}(f(\\mathbf r(t)))\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the fundamental theorem of calculus, this is equal to \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nThis concludes the proof in the case where $C$ is smooth. It's easy to show that the theorem also holds for piecewise smooth curves.\n\nFor piecewise smooth curves, let [math]C = C_1\\cup C_2 \\cup \\ldots C_n,[/math] where each [math]C_i[/math] is smooth. Then, we have\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\require{cancel}\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r \n\n&=\n\n\\int_{C_1} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r + \\int_{C_2} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r + \\cdots + \\int_{C_n} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = f(\\mathbf r(a_1)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)) + f(\\mathbf r(a_2)) - f(\\mathbf r(a_1)) + \\cdots + f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a_{n-1})) \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\cancel{f(\\mathbf r(a_1))} - f(\\mathbf r(a)) + \\cancel{f(\\mathbf r(a_2))} - \\cancel{f(\\mathbf r(a_1))} + \\cdots + f(\\mathbf r(b)) - \\cancel{f(\\mathbf r(a_{n-1}))} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&=f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n\\end{align*}" 13194139534230 17592186045417 false] [17592186135128 1064 "Here, we prove the fundamental theorem of line integrals in the two-dimensional case. The three-dimensional case is similar.\n\nLet $C$ be a smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function of two variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ We wish to show that\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nUsing our usual formula for calculating line integrals, we can write the left-hand side as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}∇f\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the integrand above is simply the derivative of $f(\\mathbf r(t))$ with respect to $t.$ Thus, we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}(f(\\mathbf r(t)))\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the fundamental theorem of calculus, this is equal to \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nThis concludes the proof in the case where $C$ is smooth. It's easy to show that the theorem also holds for piecewise smooth curves." 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415067 1080 "8" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415067 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415067