ref 17592186045493 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415100 1077 "selection" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415100 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415078 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415100 1079 #uuid "8d412271-dad8-50a6-a5b4-2d3051bb421a" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415101 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415069 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415101 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415077 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415101 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415082 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415101 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415087 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415101 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415091 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415101 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415101 1077 "selection" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415101 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415091 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415101 1079 #uuid "ab184202-13b3-5378-a512-5889441d45af" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415073 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415074 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415086 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415089 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415093 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415102 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415102 1077 "selection" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415102 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415074 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415102 1079 #uuid "f6b783c0-b546-54f4-ac0c-0b114a515648" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415098 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415103 1069 "q-104638" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415103 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,{t}^{2}-1,{t}^{2}-2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415103 1072 #uuid "03426578-7377-543b-bd88-93276fa901ef" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415103 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨5,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,-1,0)$ and $(5,3,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(5,3,0)-f(1,-1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{5(0)}-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415101 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415104 1069 "q-104624" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415104 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=y+{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415104 1072 #uuid "6e8a51b9-ad21-56cd-a267-db39e81294ac" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415104 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =(3+{4}^{2})-(-1+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3+16)-(-1) \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415097 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415105 1069 "q-104732" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415105 1071 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t,\\frac{\\pi t}{2}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y)\\,i-({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415105 1072 #uuid "9399f137-a4f5-51b7-a84e-790d740d6a70" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415105 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415102 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415106 1069 "q-104639" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415106 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{4},{t}^{6}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415106 1072 #uuid "a59364c1-7975-54ce-83e7-b32f8ae82486" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415106 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,1)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{3})-(0\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{3}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415099 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415107 1069 "q-104637" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415107 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415107 1072 #uuid "f3d23b94-95d1-5af0-9020-b1e5d5ab8286" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true] [17592186415107 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f((r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(0,2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{0(2)}-0\\cdot (1)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534230 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-00980506-979a-5a3c-bb6b-3b50f009bfac" 17592186415067 "answer-10c8a508-1d4a-574f-bb45-6efb1ced71fc" 17592186415068 "answer-12811091-da24-5f9e-a3cc-d521c187f782" 17592186415069 "answer-1aeef040-2421-5b1c-b55a-b92ee77dadcc" 17592186415070 "answer-1e6f935a-7227-56de-9d74-d58f3261b90a" 17592186415071 "answer-29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" 17592186415072 "answer-2ceeb53b-391b-5fef-9ab5-1097ef938825" 17592186415073 "answer-2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" 17592186415074 "answer-376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" 17592186415075 "answer-47d59bce-11d1-59ca-a980-8662920cfd55" 17592186415076 "answer-48539c5b-fd63-583d-8da2-566a4dd6e0bb" 17592186415077 "answer-5daedb86-7dd6-576d-aff0-8f7b1f0062ec" 17592186415078 "answer-62d13908-df4e-538d-9943-7c76652cbac9" 17592186415079 "answer-75de655b-bac7-5de2-9e4c-ce56729e4a3e" 17592186415080 "answer-87f317ce-1ebd-53fe-bd1c-2786b6ebd37d" 17592186415081 "answer-a1d2cb1e-5c01-5454-b9f6-ddf29ce394b3" 17592186415082 "answer-a2214f1f-3631-5be2-85e4-d5102a1c44c0" 17592186415083 "answer-aad335b1-b4de-568f-b2e2-0f2fa5a05048" 17592186415084 "answer-b5a24286-6aba-57bd-af78-4a37875e9372" 17592186415085 "answer-b6b875c4-e194-5a11-823c-e0a27f17aa6c" 17592186415086 "answer-b864bbf5-46ca-5a26-aa58-751cd0107a9b" 17592186415087 "answer-c1ede2a6-76f1-5387-b6b8-e8fb84166eba" 17592186415088 "answer-cc5489e8-7985-5f2a-b47e-2c5f4e8a4b10" 17592186415089 "answer-cdbbdd8b-85ea-5741-9623-bb79a02c5a1c" 17592186415090 "answer-cdf966f8-fa1b-5eaf-ab84-b21f9b032f94" 17592186415091 "answer-d6de32bd-280a-5c5a-aa34-19f1b3faadf0" 17592186415092 "answer-d782c18b-724f-503c-9bd1-3a2cfd6e6827" 17592186415093 "answer-d992fc1d-6425-5951-9f5f-b0d131ab532a" 17592186415094 "answer-db14b874-47ec-5b46-a18d-4f53face6c8f" 17592186415095 "answer-e72ad3e5-d9a9-5e06-b65e-7fe892762e86" 17592186415096 "answer-fiel